Engobe plugins are NuGet packages that add content such as shortcodes, slot templates, and static
assets. A plugin package must carry the `engobe-plugin` tag and include `content/plugin.yaml`.

## Find and inspect a plugin

Search NuGet for compatible packages, then inspect a package before installing it:

```bash
engobe plugin search email-protect
engobe plugin info Engobe.Plugin.EmailProtect
```

Plugin content can add arbitrary HTML and JavaScript to generated pages. Review package contents
and trust information before installation. Engobe requires confirmation before installing a
community package unless `--yes` is explicitly supplied.

## Install and manage plugins

```bash
engobe plugin add Engobe.Plugin.EmailProtect --version <available-version>
engobe plugin list
engobe plugin update email-protect
engobe plugin remove email-protect
```

The package manifest identifies the plugin and its declared content features:

```yaml
name: email-protect
version: 1.0.0
description: Protect email addresses from scrapers.
slots:
  - body_end
shortcodes:
  - email
```

The package can provide the matching files under `content/slots/`, `content/shortcodes/`, and
`content/static/`. Engobe installs those files in the project's `plugins/` directory.

## Lock data and local edits

NuGet installations are recorded in `.engobe/plugins.lock.json` with the package ID, version,
source, trust level, and content hash. The lock lets `engobe plugin update` resolve the installed
package without guessing. Commit this file with the project.

If you customize installed plugin files, back them up before reinstalling or updating. `--force`
can overwrite local changes. Reapply reviewed customizations after installing the package version
you intend to use.
